  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 502 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nToday: Fog This Morning, Dry and Seasonal \n\nWith a surface high overhead, light and variable winds remain over \nthe region through through this evening and into the early \novernight. Locations that received rainfall yesterday morning and \nTuesday night, are the prime areas where most guidance has fog \ndeveloping early this morning. As a result of the light winds \nand moisture from the past two days, dense fog is ongoing across\nportions of northeast Iowa, limited farther north by weak winds\nat ARX VWP observations. This fog will clear out by the mid- \nmorning. As the high remains overhead during the day, mostly \nclear skies are expected with high temperatures rising back into\nthe upper 70s to mid 80s.\n\nFriday - Midweek: Periodic Storms\n\nNorthwest flow aloft continues from through early next week as a \nresult of a longwave trough sitting over southern Canada. Multiple \nperturbations eject from this trough and move through the Upper \nMidwest starting Friday afternoon and continuing through early next \nweek. The baroclinic zone looks to be remain somewhere between Iowa \nand southern Minnesota during this period, oscillating periodically \nwith each passing wave of storms. The exact position of this \nboundary will dictate the track of our storms and how the next day's \nstorms will evolve. Given that this boundary is not expected to move \na whole lot over the next few days, we could see a heavy \nrain/flooding threat. Looking at the individual ensemble members, \nthey are not terribly excited for a lot of rainfall as the highest \namounts are just a few members with over 2\" through Tuesday. There \nis also not an anomalously moist airmass in place as PWATs should \nstay between 1.5 and 2\" during this period. While these ingredients \ndo not spell out a flooding or heavy rain threat, since some \nlocations could see a few storms or storm complexes move through, \nthe potential is there, however it is too early to say where exactly \nthe highest flooding potential would be at.\n\nWith the jet stream sagging southward a bit, shear will increase \nthis weekend into early next week. This in turn increases the risk \nfor more organized and stronger convection. Similar to the heavy \nrain threat, the exact location for where the severe weather risk \nwill be at remains uncertain. There is a little more confidence in \nFriday's severe weather potential as a cold front moves though the \nregion during the afternoon and evening. Ahead of the boundary, \nMLCAPE values of 1500 to 2000 J/kg and 0-6km shear of around 40 kts \nis expected. Combining this with an increasing low-level jet, around \n35 to 45 kts at 850mb, strong to severe storms will be possible, \nhowever the coverage of storms is in question. With these parameters \nin place, SPC has gone ahead and issued a marginal risk for severe \nweather given this potential. As there is a storm risk for much of \nthe weekend through early next week, high temperatures have come \ndown a few degrees and remain at or slightly cooler than average.",
